The known characterization is stronger than any single one of (A), (B), or (C) alone: for a diffuse separable \(\mathrm W^*\)-probability space \((M,\varphi)\), selflessness is equivalent to several conditions, including:

- existence of some nontrivial \((N,\psi)\) with \(M \subset M * N\) existentially closed;
- in fact, for every nontrivial \((N,\psi)\), the inclusion \(M \subset M * N\) is existentially closed;
- equivalently, \((M,\varphi) * (\mathrm L(\mathbb Z),\tau_{\mathbb Z})\) is selfless.

So the best choice is the meta-statement that a stronger theorem proves more than a single one of the listed alternatives.

\boxed{D}
